WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.310
Welcome to working backwards.

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:05.620
Some problems tell you how a value changed but hide

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:06.690
the starting point.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.000
As soon as you know where the story ends, you can walk

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.710
back along its steps if each change can be undone.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.870
An AMC question can describe a number machine,

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.850
a shrinking pile, or money spent in stages.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.020
A Rossmoyne style puzzle may ask you to explain why the

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.430
starting amount is forced.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.770
Reverse the steps and replay them forward as a check.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.330
A number machine multiplies the input by four.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:33.690
Then it adds seven.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.250
Its output is thirty-one.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:36.870
What was the input?

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.870
Ask yourself which step touched the result last.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.690
The last forward step added seven.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:47.050
Undo it first.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:49.830
Thirty-one minus seven is twenty-four.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:54.130
Then undo multiplying by four by dividing by four.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:55.670
The input is six.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.150
Run six forward.

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:00.460
Four times six is twenty-four, and adding seven brings

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:01.250
us to thirty-one.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.430
Sam gives away one third of her stamps.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:07.440
Later she receives seven stamps and ends

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:08.270
up with twenty-five.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.530
How many did she have at the start?

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.360
After undoing the gift, think about which fraction of

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.230
the original pile is still there.

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:21.740
Remove the seven received stamps.

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:24.540
Eighteen stamps were left before the gift.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:27.360
That is two of the original three equal parts.

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:31.280
Eighteen divided by two is nine stamps per part.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:33.280
Three parts is twenty-seven.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:37.000
Check by giving away nine and then adding seven.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.700
A player stops at three stations.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.830
At each station they hand over half the tickets they

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:45.200
have at that moment, and then two more tickets.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:48.420
After the third station they have five tickets.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:50.380
How many did they start with?

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.490
Start at the third station.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:57.490
Add back the last two tickets.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:01.050
Seven tickets were left just after the pile was halved.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:03.010
Double seven to restore fourteen.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.110
That was the pile on arrival at the third station.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:08.550
You can use the same reverse rule again.

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:12.510
At the second station, fourteen plus two is sixteen.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:14.550
Double it to get thirty-two.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:16.310
At the first station,

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.510
thirty-two plus two is thirty-four.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:20.590
Double it to get sixty-eight.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:21.920
Check forward.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:24.230
Halve and subtract two three times,

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.730
passing through thirty-two, fourteen and finally five.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:29.550
Watch the order.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:32.810
Doubling five and then adding two gives twelve.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.370
When twelve goes forward through the station,

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.010
the result is four, so that route fails.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.070
Also watch what the fraction is taken from.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.030
Each station uses the pile currently in front of it.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:47.270
On an exam, circle what you know at the end.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.900
Draw the forward arrows, and write the inverse

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:51.610
operations on the way back.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.210
Label what each fraction refers to.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.080
Then run your starting value forward through the story

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:58.490
to check every stage.
